+3. **Write-after-write (same file):** A's `writes` set and B's `writes` set share at least one path. Under the default `--overlap serialize`, concurrent writes to the same file are never safe; serialize them in document order (A before B if A appears first in the plan). The overlap set is `writes ∪ Test:` paths on both sides (see the reads bullet). Under `--overlap fold` this tier does not create the edge for an *eligible* pair — the two tasks share a wave and the engine folds their same-file edits at merge time, with the runtime fold predicate authoritative; the drop happens at construction (the pair is recorded in `dropped_pairs`, both orderings) so the `ambiguous-files` and catch-all tiers below still read a truthful adjacency. A pair is droppable only where this tier would have created a NEW edge: forward document order, no edge already recorded for the pair (a `marker`, `text`, `interface`, or semantic edge keeps it serialized), and not cycle-blocked. Every other `why` label survives fold untouched. Eligibility is decided by the `--repo-root <path>` pre-filter (hermetic, memoised per path): a pair keeps its edge when any path in its overlap set, resolved against the root and existing there, is non-text, over `RESOLVER_LINE_CAP` lines (via the kernel's `split_lines`), or a symlink (`Path.is_symlink()`; gitlinks are left to the runtime guard). Without `--repo-root` the pre-filter is inert — every pair is eligible. Each ineligible path is recorded once in `marker_conflicts` as a `kind: "inference"` entry (`task: ""`, `edge: "<path>"`) — no new diagnostic vocabulary, the same bucket `Depends-on: none overridden` uses.
